WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.953
Every oscillating mechanical system has ways
it naturally prefers to move.

2
00:00:04.953 --> 00:00:09.768
Push a swing slowly, or far too quickly, and
the motion stays modest.

3
00:00:09.768 --> 00:00:15.312
Push near its natural rhythm and each input
can reinforce the motion already underway.

4
00:00:15.312 --> 00:00:20.081
That frequency-sensitive amplification is the
core idea behind resonance.

5
00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:25.412
A frequency-response plot puts driving
frequency on the horizontal axis and response

6
00:00:25.412 --> 00:00:27.589
amplitude on the vertical axis.

7
00:00:27.589 --> 00:00:29.707
The peak marks the resonant region.

8
00:00:29.707 --> 00:00:35.298
Its location and width tell engineers which
excitations are most dangerous or most useful.

9
00:00:35.298 --> 00:00:41.212
A design does not merely ask whether a force
exists; it asks how often that force repeats.

10
00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:46.847
Real systems lose energy through damping, so
their response does not grow without bound.

11
00:00:46.847 --> 00:00:53.135
Engineers can add damping, change stiffness or
mass, or keep operating speeds away from a

12
00:00:53.135 --> 00:00:54.393
troublesome natural frequency.

13
00:00:54.393 --> 00:00:59.611
The practical lesson is simple: resonance is
not automatically failure, but ignoring the

14
00:00:59.611 --> 00:01:04.428
frequency relationship can turn a small
repeating force into a large motion.
